<node id="11137449" title="Re^3: Splitting in while loop" created="2021-10-12 12:41:26" updated="2021-10-12 12:41:26" _doctext_mode="strict"><type id="11">note</type>
<author id="830549">haukex</author>
<doctext field_name="doctext">&lt;blockquote&gt;&lt;i&gt;&lt;c&gt;while (my $line = &lt;&gt;)&lt;/c&gt; ... Similarly, ... &lt;c&gt;while (my $item = pop @my_array)&lt;/c&gt;&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;c&gt;
use warnings;
use strict;

while (my $line = &lt;DATA&gt;) {
	chomp $line;
	print "&lt;$line&gt;\n";
}

my @array = ("Foo","0","Bar");
while (my $item = pop @array) {
	print "[$item]\n";
}

__DATA__
Hello
0

World
&lt;/c&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Outputs:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;c&gt;
&lt;Hello&gt;
&lt;0&gt;
&lt;&gt;
&lt;World&gt;
[Bar]
&lt;/c&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I'd call that a pretty serious caveat. As per [doc://perlop#I/O-Operators], the first is equivalent to &lt;c&gt;while ( defined( my $line = &lt;&gt; ) )&lt;/c&gt;, and [doc://readline] returns &lt;c&gt;undef&lt;/c&gt; at EOF, while the second one will stop at &lt;i&gt;any&lt;/i&gt; [id://11104607|false] value, and arrays can contain &lt;c&gt;undef&lt;/c&gt;s too.&lt;/p&gt;</doctext>
